Apple has updated its U.S. <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/trade-in">trade-in estimates</a>, cutting values for every eligible iPhone while raising them across the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> and Mac lineups.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/Apple-Trade-Ins-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1065166" /><br/>
The refresh follows the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-event-live-blog-september-2026/">announcement</a> of the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-18-pro/">iPhone 18 Pro</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>. Their predecessors now appear on Apple's trade-in list for the first time, led by the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17-pro/">iPhone 17 Pro</a> Max at up to &#36;885. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-air/">iPhone Air</a> is valued at &#36;585, exactly the same as the standard <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a>, despite having launched at a &#36;200 higher price:<br/>
<br/>
<ul><br/>
<li><strong>iPhone 17 Pro Max</strong>: &#36;885</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPhone 17 Pro</strong>: &#36;785</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPhone Air</strong>: &#36;585</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPhone 17</strong>: &#36;585</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<br/>
The update is also the first to run under Apple's new condition grading system, which is now live. Devices with minor cosmetic defects such as small chips in the display glass were previously treated much like those with far more serious damage, resulting in a similarly steep cut to their value. As we <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/09/apple-to-offer-better-trade-in-pricing/">reported</a> last week, Apple now applies more granular tiers that better reflect a device's actual condition, and no device receives less than it would have under the old system.<br/>
<br/>
